  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1204 AM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAs the upper level trough moves northeast out of the state, upper \nlevel ridging begins to build to the west. This building ridge of \nhigh pressure will allow for temperatures to warm back up, with \nhighs today in the mid 70s to mid 80s. On Tuesday, the ridge will \nbegin to propagate eastward with a negative tilt, creating \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Embedded shortwaves within this flow will \ncreate enough instability for the development of afternoon/evening \nthunderstorms, mainly in Southwest MT. Increased moisture aloft will \nresult in chances for some showers in the afternoon/evening hours in \nSouthwest MT as well. \n\nOn Wednesday, the upper level ridge exits the region, and an upper \nlevel trough swing northeastward from Northern California into the \nNorthern Rockies. This will allow for shower and thunderstorm \nchances to become more widespread, as well as strong southwesterly \nwinds to form. On Thursday, this smaller upper level trough will \ngive way to a much larger upper level trough forming over the \nOlympic Peninsula, allowing once again for widespread shower and \nthunderstorm chances and strong southwesterly winds. \n\nLooking into Friday and beyond, conditions will depend of what this \nlarger upper level trough does. There remains inconsistency in what \nthe models are predicting for the path of this trough, as outlined \nin the Forecast Confidence and Scenarios section below. -Dzomba\n\nForecast Confidence and Scenarios:\n\nWinds Wednesday and Thursday: as a series of upper level troughs \nembedded in persistent upper level southwesterly flow moves \noverhead, winds will increase, primarily across Southwestern Montana \nWednesday and Thursday. While the wind increase looks likely, the \nmagnitude, extent, and duration of the winds are likely to be quite \nsensitive to the exact track of these disturbances. As of Sunday, \nthe NBM probabilities seem to indicate that there are roughly \nsimilar probabilities for wind gusts over 45 mph, with chances \naround 50% generally confined to some of the North/South oriented \nvalleys of Southwestern Montana near and south of Interstate 15. \n\nNext Weekend: There are a few ways next weekend could pan out, \nlargely driven by how strong an upper level trough gets over the \nwest coast and whether or not it \u201ccuts off\u201d from the flow and \nbecomes a closed upper level low. There does seem to be a little \nmore support for the latter scenario (around 75% of the ensemble \nmembers), but its strength and location will play a large role in \nthe details of our weather, and models seem to be in quite a bit of \ndisagreement with these particulars. Should the low/trough wind up \nstaying offshore, next weekend is likely to be a bit on the warmer \nside of the forecast, with upper level ridging building in overhead. \nHowever, a more eastward placement of this low/trough, which is \naround 60% of the ensemble members would result in a cooler, more \nunsettled result for next weekend\u2019s weather. Ludwig",
